**Fulcrum position label**

The knife-edge stand now carries an `x = 50.0 cm` label in the same style as the hangers' position labels — same 19 px muted text on desktop, 25 px on phones, centred on the pivot.

- It sits 24 units under the stand's base (baseline at y = 272 of the 500-unit viewBox), so it clears the base and still ends well above the hanging blocks, which start at y = 300. Even a hanger parked right at the fulcrum doesn't collide with it.
- It re-renders with the board, so it tracks the pivot live: dragging the fulcrum from 50 cm read `x = 58.5 cm` mid-drag, matching the `fulcrum 58.5 cm` chip and the fine-move bar in the same frame. Loading Case 5 (pivot 40) showed `x = 40.0 cm`.
- At the stick's ends it stays inside the viewBox (centred at 74 and 926 of 1000).

**Randomised stick mass**

- New `randomStickMass()` returns a value from **88.5 to 90.5 g** to 0.1 g, so it looks like a real weighing rather than a round 150 g.
- It is drawn once at start-up. Four fresh starts gave 89.9, 89.6, 89.2 and 89.5 g, all in range, all one decimal.
- It is then part of the saved state: a reload kept 89.9, and switching to Case 5 kept 89.6 rather than reverting. Loading a case no longer overwrites the stick's mass — I removed the `stickMass` field from all ten case presets and the two places that copied it in, since the stick is one physical object for the session. Apparatus → Stick mass still accepts any value you type.
- The data table's stick row picks it up automatically: `meterstick at CG 89.6 50.0 10.0 CW 0.088`.

**Verified:** main script parses (6 400-line block), no console errors, the Case 1 fresh state opens with the fulcrum label and a random mass, the fine-move bar still works on the fulcrum, and there's no horizontal scroll at 390 or 1366. I cleared the saved state at the end, so the app opens fresh — that last reload rolled 89.5 g. [README.md](torque-balance/README.md) documents both changes. Nothing is committed.

One consequence worth knowing: because a case no longer sets the stick mass, the figure you see in Case 5's centre-of-gravity work is the randomised one rather than a fixed 150 g — the arithmetic is unaffected, but any answer key you've written against 150 g will differ.
