Fixed in [torque-balance/index.html](torque-balance/index.html), and the checked page is open in the Preview tab.

**What was actually stale**

The app has no separate answer sheet — its answer key is the numbers baked into the case presets. All of them except one are just starting poses. The exception was **Case 6(d)'s opening pivot of 44.3 cm**, which is Case 6(c)'s balance point *for a 150 g stick*: with m₁ = 100 g at 0 cm, m₂ = 100 g at 80 cm and the stick at 150 g the balance solves to 44.3, and 6(d) is written to open "where 6(c) balanced" before m₂ moves to 90 cm. Once the stick's mass became 88.5–90.5 g, 6(c)'s balance is 43.1, so 6(d) was starting 1.2 cm away from the answer it claims to sit on — and the difference would show up in the recorded x₀′ values across 6(a)–6(d).

**The fix**

- New `balancePivot(setup, pairs)` solves the support position for a set of hanging masses plus the stick itself, using the case's own centre of gravity.
- Case 6(d)'s `pivot` is now a function of the setup instead of a literal, and `loadCase` accepts a computed pivot (it now applies the CG and clamp mass before the pivot so the calculation is well-defined).
- **Reset this case** goes through the same path, so it also lands on the computed answer.

**Measured**

| Stick mass | 6(c) balance (*Level it*) | 6(d) starts at | 6(d)'s prediction answer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 89.5 g (fresh run) | 43.1 cm | **43.1 cm** (was 44.3) | 46.5 cm |
| 89.9 g | 43.1 cm | 43.1 cm | 46.6 cm |
| 88.5 g | 43.1 cm | 43.1 cm | 46.5 cm |
| 150 g (typed) | 44.3 cm | 44.3 cm | 47.1 cm |

That 150 g row is the proof the old constant was 6(c)'s answer: the computed pivot reproduces 44.3 cm exactly, but only for a 150 g stick. The prediction check also agrees with itself — entering 46.5 cm on the 89.5 g rig returned *"balances at 46.5 cm — 0.1% off ✓"*, while the old 44.3 read *4.8% off*, and 6(d) still opens visibly unbalanced (Στ 0.422 vs 0.520 N·m, tilting clockwise) as its first step requires.

Cases 1, 2(a), 2(b), 3 and 5 were already safe: their pivots sit at the stick's centre of gravity, where the stick contributes no torque, and their prediction answers (lever arm, unknown mass) are solved from the live masses. No console errors, script parses, and I cleared the saved state afterwards so the app opens in Case 1 with a fresh random stick mass. [README.md](torque-balance/README.md) notes that 6(d) derives its opening pose from the stick's mass. Nothing is committed.
